  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 525 AM EDT TUE JUL 21 2026\n\nThe models remain in generally good agreement with an amplified \nand stagnant long wave pattern to rule over the CONUS through \nearly next week. The center of a seasonably strong upper level \nridge will meander/retrograde northwest from the southern Plains \nto the Four Corners region and building into parts of the Central\nPlains to Lower MS Valley at times, while broader troughing \nmodulates east of the Mississippi River with it's axis aligned\nfrom near the Ontario and Quebec border to the mid Atlantic coast\nto east of the southeast US coast. This will result in a reprieve\nfrom the humidity across the Ohio and Tennessee valleys at midweek\ninto early Friday. However, humidity will be on the increase once\nagain across the region by Friday afternoon and into early next \nweek.\n\nBy Wednesday evening, a surface cold front should have pushed\nwell into VA to the Smokes to the Lower MS Valley, and high\npressure building into the Lower OH Valley form the Upper MS\nValley. Wednesday night, welcomed northeasterly low level winds \nushering in much lower dew points than has been experienced this\nmonth. Some fog will likely on Wednesday night into early Thursday\nmorning and some of this could be dense in some more southern\nlocales. However, although the pressure gradient/mixiness may \nlimit this coverage. Drier and cooler weather will be in full \nswing on Thursday, with valley fog favored with high pressure\ndominating on Thursday night.\n\nAs troughing sharpens back up over the Ohio Valley Friday to\nSaturday, shortwaves moving southeast within the established \nnorthwesterly flow aloft between the trough axis to the east and\nnortheast and the ridging in the western to south-central Conus. \nModel details regarding timing remain lower confidence this far \nout, but rain chances will return as some moderate moisture \nadvection occurs in the low levels, with LREF mean PWATs of 1.4 to\n1.5 inches nosing back into southeastern portions of the \nCommonwealth by Friday. PoPs look to peak Friday night into \nSaturday, with somewhat better model agreement on increased mid \nand upper level forcing in place from one of the shortwaves. For \nthe rest of the weekend and early next week, despite the favorable\nstormier track that northwest flow can mean aloft, mean PWATS \nright now remain in the 50th to 75th percentile, so only slight to\nchance PoPs are in the forecast for Sunday through Monday. \n\nHighs will remain cooler than normal by about 5 degrees on \nThursday, before gradually warming to the mid to upper 80s by \nSUnday Monday. Overnight lows will moderate from the upper 50s \nand low 60s Wednesday and Thursday nights, to the upper 60s and \naround 70 by early next week.",
